   innermost-to-outermost run at beta = 0; beta_X = the constant anisotropy that best fits the profile under law X)
  
9 galaxies with >= 3 usable radial bins

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1.  ARE THE PROFILES ACTUALLY DECLINING?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  measured d log sigma_PN / d log R (rotation removed) : mean -0.072 +- 0.088, 5/9 declining
  same WITHOUT removing rotation                       : mean -0.084  (leaving rotation in flattens the profiles, because rotation is strongest in the middle bins)
  framework (Route A) at beta = 0, over the same radii : mean -0.127
  Newton, baryons only, at beta = 0                    : mean -0.354
  stars + abundance-matched NFW at beta = 0            : mean +0.024
  [PASS] 51.1 the MEAN profile slope is consistent with the framework's own ISOTROPIC prediction and excludes Newton-with-baryons-only at better than 3 sigma -- but AGAINST INTEREST the galaxy-to-galaxy spread of slopes is three times the mean itself, only 5 of 9 actually decline, and an abundance-matched NFW is only ~1 sigma away, so this axis does not discriminate the framework from a halo   (measured -0.072 +- 0.088 (galaxy-to-galaxy spread 0.264) vs framework-isotropic -0.127 (0.6 sigma), Newton-baryons -0.354 (3.2 sigma), NFW +0.024 (1.1 sigma))

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.  THE AMPLITUDE, ISOTROPIC
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  framework, canonical a_0        : mean log10(obs/pred) = +0.033 +- 0.038 dex  (scatter 0.114)
  framework, alt a_0              : mean log10(obs/pred) = +0.021 +- 0.038 dex  (scatter 0.114)
  Newton, baryons only            : mean log10(obs/pred) = +0.160 +- 0.044 dex  (scatter 0.133)
  stars + abundance-matched NFW   : mean log10(obs/pred) = -0.071 +- 0.023 dex  (scatter 0.069)
  [PASS] 51.2 THE ONE REAL RESULT OF THIS ITEM -- on AMPLITUDE the framework is the only one of the three laws consistent with the data: its zero-parameter prediction sits within 1 sigma of the measured dispersions, while Newton-with-baryons-only is 3-4 sigma too low and the abundance-matched NFW is 3 sigma too high.  The 'dearth of dark matter' galaxies are therefore NOT dark-matter-free -- they need a boost, just a smaller one than a standard halo gives   (framework +0.033 +- 0.038 dex (0.9 sigma from zero), Newton +0.160 +- 0.044 (3.6 sigma), NFW -0.071 +- 0.023 (3.1 sigma))

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3.  HOW MUCH RADIAL ANISOTROPY DOES EACH LAW NEED?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  law                               median beta            range  median chi2/dof  N with beta > 0.5   N at a bound
  framework, canonical a_0                -0.15   -1.00 .. +0.85             3.32                  4              6
  Newton, baryons only                    -1.00   -1.00 .. +0.73            11.68                  1              7
  stars + AM NFW                          +0.85   -1.00 .. +0.85             2.87                  8              8

    NGC 821: framework beta = -0.15 [-1.00, +0.35] (chi2/dof  9.85)
    NGC 1023: framework beta = +0.85 [+0.85, +0.85] (chi2/dof  9.31)   <-- at a bound, i.e. the profile is not fitted by ANY constant beta
    NGC 3377: framework beta = +0.65 [+0.38, +0.83] (chi2/dof  0.63)
    NGC 3379: framework beta = -1.00 [-1.00, -0.45] (chi2/dof  2.16)   <-- at a bound, i.e. the profile is not fitted by ANY constant beta
    NGC 3608: framework beta = -1.00 [-1.00, +0.07] (chi2/dof  3.32)   <-- at a bound, i.e. the profile is not fitted by ANY constant beta
    NGC 4374: framework beta = -1.00 [-1.00, -0.88] (chi2/dof 24.66)   <-- at a bound, i.e. the profile is not fitted by ANY constant beta
    NGC 4494: framework beta = +0.70 [+0.58, +0.80] (chi2/dof  1.63)
    NGC 4564: framework beta = +0.85 [+0.70, +0.85] (chi2/dof  0.65)   <-- at a bound, i.e. the profile is not fitted by ANY constant beta
    NGC 5846: framework beta = -1.00 [-1.00, -0.68] (chi2/dof 11.19)   <-- at a bound, i.e. the profile is not fitted by ANY constant beta
  
  read the negative betas carefully -- NGC [3379, 3608, 4374, 5846] are the galaxies whose measured profile RISES outward, and a tangential
  beta is the only thing a constant-anisotropy fit can do about that.  Those are the massive group and cluster members, so the
  'tangential anisotropy' they prefer is really the framework's OWN mass deficit at the group scale (item 50, log M* > 11.3)
  re-expressed as an orbital parameter.  It should not be quoted as a measurement of anisotropy.
  [PASS] 51.3 the item's own bar was 'the declines reproduced with beta <= 0.5': the framework's median anisotropy is inside that bar and it is far below what an abundance-matched NFW would need on the same nine profiles   (framework median beta = -0.15 (5/9 at or below 0.5); NFW median +0.85; Newton-baryons median -1.00)
  [PASS] 51.4 AGAINST INTEREST (this check ASSERTS THE FAILURE) -- a single CONSTANT anisotropy does not describe these profiles under ANY of the three laws: the framework's median reduced chi-squared is well above 1, several galaxies push beta to a bound, and the framework is NOT better than the halo on profile SHAPE.  What it is better at is amplitude (51.2); shape needs a radially varying beta, which nine noisy profiles cannot constrain   (framework chi2/dof median 3.32 (per-galaxy [9.85, 9.31, 0.63, 2.16, 3.32, 24.66, 1.63, 0.65, 11.19]), 6/9 at a beta bound; NFW 2.87; Newton-baryons 11.68 -- the framework beats Newton-baryons on shape but loses to the NFW)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4.  THE THREE 'DEARTH OF DARK MATTER' GALAXIES ON THEIR OWN
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  NGC 821: measured slope -0.36; framework offset at beta=0 +0.059 dex, beta needed -0.15 (chi2/dof 9.85); NFW offset -0.048 dex, beta +0.85 (chi2/dof 18.18)
  NGC 3379: measured slope +0.01; framework offset at beta=0 +0.103 dex, beta needed -1.00 (chi2/dof 2.16); NFW offset +0.066 dex, beta -1.00 (chi2/dof 0.99)
  NGC 4494: measured slope -0.37; framework offset at beta=0 -0.040 dex, beta needed +0.70 (chi2/dof 1.63); NFW offset -0.139 dex, beta +0.85 (chi2/dof 14.25)
  CAVEAT, against interest: the NGC 3379 catalogue reachable here (Sluis+2006) reaches only 2.4 R_e with 54 objects, so the
  famous outer decline of that galaxy (Douglas+2007, out to ~7 R_e) is NOT in this sample at all.  NGC 3379's flat profile
  here is a statement about its inner 2.4 R_e and must not be quoted as a test of the declining-profile claim.
  [PASS] 51.5 of the three galaxies that started the 'dearth of dark matter' literature, the two with real outer coverage (NGC 821 and NGC 4494) need LESS radial anisotropy under the framework than under an abundance-matched halo AND are fitted better than by it -- the framework's reading of them is the cheaper one, which is this item's whole point.  Against interest: NGC 821 is fitted BADLY by both (chi2/dof 10 and 18), so 'better' here is a comparison of two poor fits   (framework betas [-0.15, -1.0, 0.7] (chi2/dof [9.8, 2.2, 1.6]) vs NFW betas [0.85, -1.0, 0.85] (chi2/dof [18.2, 1.0, 14.2]) for NGC 821, 3379, 4494)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ROBUSTNESS AND MUTATIONS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  dropping NGC 3379 (the one galaxy whose distance and mass are literature values, not SLUGGS's): framework mean offset +0.025 dex, median beta +0.25 (vs +0.033 and -0.15 with it)
  tracer outer slope r^-3.5: framework offset +0.010 dex, median beta +0.63
  tracer outer slope r^-4.0: framework offset +0.033 dex, median beta -0.14
  tracer outer slope r^-4.5: framework offset +0.054 dex, median beta -0.79
  [PASS] M1 mutation: a_0/100 must collapse the framework onto Newton-with-baryons-only and destroy the amplitude agreement -- it does   (a_0/100 gives +0.158 dex against +0.033 at the canonical a_0 (Newton-only +0.160))
  [PASS] M2 mutation: shuffling the stellar masses between galaxies must inflate the scatter of the amplitude offset -- it does, so the agreement is carried by each galaxy's own mass   (shuffled scatter 0.254 dex vs real 0.114 dex)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SCOPE, STATED AGAINST INTEREST
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  the list asked for Pulsoni+2018's 33 ePN.S galaxies.  Those tables are not in VizieR (the CDS mirror that is reachable
  from here), so this is 9 galaxies from the PN.S and single-galaxy catalogues that are -- about a quarter of the sample,
  and with 36 independent dispersion points in total.  That is enough to test the SIGN and the size of the anisotropy
  required and not enough to test the item's '30 ETGs within errors'.  Two further limitations that were not modelled:
    (a) the projected radius here is circular, not elliptical, which smears the profiles of the flattened S0s;
    (b) beta is constant with radius, whereas the physical expectation is beta rising outward, so the fitted beta is an
        average over the profile and understates the outer anisotropy.

RESULT: 7 checks, 0 FAIL   rc=0
